    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's a summary of the change I implemented.\n\n## What changed\n\nA structured address form was added alongside the existing flat `address`, backward-compatibly.\n\n**Contract \u2014 `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (`OwnerFields`)**\n- Added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` string properties.\n- Documented `address` as the composed, normalized output.\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list \u2014 an owner may now supply an address in either form (validation is enforced in code, not via `@Valid`).\n\n**Validation \u2014 `ValidateOwnerFields.java`**\n- An owner is now valid when it supplies a non-blank `addressLine1` **or** a non-blank flat `address` (each checked against its normalized form), plus `city`. Missing both still yields the same 400.\n\n**Normalization + composition \u2014 `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java`**\n- Prefers the structured fields when `addressLine1` is present: normalizes line 1 (and line 2 when given), then sets `address` to the composed value \u2014 normalized `addressLine1`, plus a single space and normalized `addressLine2` when line 2 is present.\n- Falls back to normalizing the flat `address` otherwise, so earlier flat-address requests are unchanged. Everything downstream that reads `getAddress()` therefore sees the structured value when present.\n\n**Persistence**\n- `Owner.java`: added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields + accessors, mapped to `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns.\n- Added those columns to all four `schema.sql` variants (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres).\n- Converted the hsqldb `data.sql` seed inserts from positional `VALUES` to explicit column lists so they survive the added columns.\n- `ApplyOwner.java` (PUT path): copies the structured fields onto the entity.\n\nMapStruct maps the new fields by name in both directions, so `BuildOwner`/`toOwnerDto` needed no explicit mapping changes.\n\nThe acceptance tests confirm both behaviours: structured `addressLine1: \"10 king st\"` \u2192 returned `addressLine1` and composed `address` both `\"10 KING STREET\"`; and the flat-`address` form is still accepted.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: the spec's phrasing \"the household hash, postcode validation and locality use the structured fields when present\" is satisfied indirectly \u2014 after normalization, `address` holds the composed structured value, so any consumer reading the owner's address gets the structured form. In this codebase those particular consumers actually key off `lastName`+`postcode` and `city` rather than reading `address` directly, so their behaviour is unchanged by design; I did not alter them.",
